  LITERATURE STATUS (cited, not proved here): the un-averaged two-point
  Chowla statement sum_{n<=x} lambda(n)lambda(n+2) = o(x) is OPEN.
    * Tao, arXiv:1509.05422 (Forum of Math. Pi, 2016) proves the
      LOGARITHMICALLY averaged version and states the un-averaged
      two-point Chowla conjecture as the open assertion it approximates;
      he describes the log-averaged result as breaking the parity barrier
      for that problem, which is exactly the barrier the un-averaged
      problem retains.
    * Matomaki-Radziwill (2015) and Matomaki-Radziwill-Tao (2015):
      cancellation for almost all shifts h, not the fixed h=2.
    * Tao, arXiv:1605.04628: equivalence of the log-averaged Chowla and
      Sarnak conjectures (again log-averaged).
    * Tao-Teravainen, arXiv:1809.02518: correlations at almost all scales;
      still averaged in the scale/shift.
    No source claims the un-averaged two-point case.

==============================================================================
VERDICT ON THE BARRIER
==============================================================================
C1  factual legs CONFIRMED, two corrections:
    - leg 1 (no arithmetic input): confirmed -- the word is a function of
      the target only, identical prefixes for targets agreeing to 1e-50,
      and it factors through sin(2*pi*theta).
    - leg 2 (budgets separate INDEX parity, not Omega parity): confirmed and
      strengthened -- the budgets are constants in p, and index parity is
      INVISIBLE to (STAR) (twist invariance, 0 mismatches on 10^6).
    - leg 3 (no Euler product): confirmed independently, and with a tail
      bound ~10^5 times tighter than the round's; the verdict is robust.
C2  D(2) = -1.12681304, naive multiplicative model = 0.88757352,
    discrepancy = 2.014387, tight rigorous tail = 1.84e-05.
C3  every attempt to build a non-null greedy-sine object:
    (a) prime-indexed alphabet WITH lambda(p+2) as the sign: non-null but
        CIRCULAR (the alphabet imports the target function; (-1)^omega(q)
        is a second, weaker import, agreeing with lambda on all squarefree
        q); the same rule with permuted signs is null.  The non-nullity
        lives in the ALPHABET, not in the greedy dynamics.
    (b) unsigned-word statistics (one-point, two-point, lambda-weighted
        support pairs, span-block density) vs size-/span-matched random
        supports: all NULL.
    (c) parity lock vs {lambda=+1}: chance-level match, and the support has
        density zero, so no positive-density matching is possible.
C4  the barrier's CONCLUSION (the goal is out of reach by these means) is
    correctly stated, but it is a HEURISTIC, not a theorem.  One factual
    error found: the quoted sparse bound 2*#{n<=x+2: n in A} is FALSE; the
    correct constant is 4 (witness A={5}, x>=5, |Delta S|=4).  The o(x)
    conclusion is unaffected.  A second inherited defect: rh46's product
    'tail bound' 1.808988 is 4*sum_(p<=1e5)1/p^2 over the RETAINED primes,
    not a bound on the omitted p>1e5 factor (<=1e-5); the no-Euler-product
    verdict survives, certified here with the correct tight bound.

IS THE BARRIER CORRECTLY STATED?  Yes in substance, with two caveats:
  (i) the literal phrase 'every greedy-sine object ... is an additive
      character in log n' is both imprecise (n^{-it} is a multiplicative
      character of (R_+,x); the budgets are constants, not characters) and
      too wide: C3a shows a greedy object of the SAME take rule but with
      arithmetic signs mixes with lambda perfectly.  The barrier must be
      read as 'for a SIGN-BLIND alphabet with no arithmetic input'.
  (ii) no proof is offered that additive-in-log-n data cannot force a
      multiplicative correlation; the document says 'no mechanism known'.
      The verified legs are the budget sign-blindness and the absence of an
      Euler product; the impossibility statement itself stays OPEN.
  Round deliverable status at audit time:
